<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    Permits plain HTTP.

    Android blocks outbound cleartext by default from targetSdk 28. That default is
    right for apps talking to the internet, but this one replicates to another till on
    the same shop LAN, addressed by whatever IP the router handed it. Those peers cannot
    be enumerated here, and they have no certificate to present.

    Without this the app can still SERVE over HTTP but cannot CALL out, so an Android
    terminal can receive replication and never send it — it could never act as primary.

    This is the same exposure recorded against step 8 in REPLICATION.md: tokens cross
    the LAN in clear text. When TLS lands, narrow this to the peers that need it rather
    than leaving it open.
-->
<network-security-config>
    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="true" />
</network-security-config>
